<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William the Conqueror</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1028–<a href="/years/1087">1087</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as William I of England, William of Normandy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William the Conqueror was Duke of Normandy from 1035 and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> from 1066, after defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold II</a> at Hastings. He crushed English revolts, replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> elite with Norman lords and created powerful frontier earldoms such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a>. He ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday survey</a> of 1086, and his conquest tied England to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> for centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror.jpg" alt="William the Conqueror" width="320" height="533" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Myrabella · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_the_Conqueror_(TFA).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
